{"name":"Purple Verbena","hex":"#46354b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"purple","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colour between red and blue; violet, though often closer to magenta.","Any non-spectral colour on the line of purples on a colour chromaticity diagram or a colour wheel between violet and red."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a purple hue.","Not predominantly red or blue, but having a mixture of Democrat and Republican support."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/purple"},{"term":"verbena","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Verbena, a genus of herbaceous plants of which several species are extensively cultivated for the great beauty of their flowers; vervain."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/verbena"}],"entries":[{"title":"Verbena","blocks":[{"text":"Verbena, also known as vervain or verveine, is a genus in the family Verbenaceae. It contains about 150 species of annual and perennial herbaceous or semi-woody flowering plants. The majority of the species are native to the Americas and Asia; however, Verbena officinalis, the common vervain or common verbena, is the type species and native to Europe."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Eisenkraut%2C_Passau.JPG/330px-Eisenkraut%2C_Passau.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Verbena","wikidata":"Q165290"},{"title":"Purple","blocks":[{"text":"Purple is a color similar in appearance to violet light. In the RYB color model historically used in the arts, purple is a secondary color created by combining red and blue pigments. In the CMYK color model used in modern printing, purple is made by combining magenta pigment with cyan pigment, black pigment, or both. In the RGB color model used in computer and television screens, purple is created by mixing red and blue light in order to create colors that appear similar to violet light. According to color theory, purple is considered a cool color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Iris_sanguinea_cultivar%2C_Wakehurst_Place%2C_UK_-_Diliff.jpg/330px-Iris_sanguinea_cultivar%2C_Wakehurst_Place%2C_UK_-_Diliff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Purple","wikidata":"Q3257809"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Verbena_officinalis_Sturm22.jpg/500px-Verbena_officinalis_Sturm22.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Verbena_officinalis_Sturm22.jpg/500px-Verbena_officinalis_Sturm22.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Botanical illustration of common vervain (V. officinalis) from Deutschlands Flora in Abbildungen by Johann Georg Sturm and Jacob Sturm, 1796","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVerbena_officinalis_Sturm22.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Verbena_bonariensis2.jpg/500px-Verbena_bonariensis2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Verbena_bonariensis2.jpg/500px-Verbena_bonariensis2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" Purpletop vervain (V. bonariensis) as an ornamental plant","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVerbena_bonariensis2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Verbena_canadensis_Homestead_Purple_5zz.jpg/500px-Verbena_canadensis_Homestead_Purple_5zz.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Verbena_canadensis_Homestead_Purple_5zz.jpg/500px-Verbena_canadensis_Homestead_Purple_5zz.jpg?utm_source=e